  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1209 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nA hot and humid day is underway as of 11 AM with air temperatures\nalready in the upper 80s and lower 90s with triple digit heat\nindex values. Expect temperatures to continue to rise this\nafternoon, into the mid to upper 90s, as ridging amplifies. A few\nshowers and thunderstorms may develop this afternoon across north\nMississippi, along a shortwave from the tropical airmass in the\nGulf. While not anticipating any severe thunderstorms with this\nactivity, hefty downpours are likely in any development.\n\nTuesday will be the hottest day of the summer as upper 90s are\nexpected across the entire region. Areas along and west of the\nriver have low, but still notable, probabilities of 100F or\nhigher air temperatures Tuesday afternoon. A few additional\ncounties will likely need to be added to the Extreme Heat Warning\nfor tomorrow. This amount of heat will aid in very steep low\nlevel lapse rates as well as SBCAPE. An approaching frontal\nboundary will sink south and spark scattered thunderstorms in its\nwake and tap into this very unstable, tropical airmass. The\nbetter height falls look to dive over southeast Kentucky into\nMiddle Tennessee, but with moisture and lift, a strong to severe\nthunderstorm cannot be ruled out tomorrow afternoon and evening.\nDamaging winds and torrential rainfall would be the primary\nthreats. The RRFS simulated radar is much more robust in\ncoverage, but has similar timing to the other CAMs, with storms\nfiring on the KY/TN border between 4-6 PM. Would not expect the\nsevere weather window to go beyond 2 AM, but showers and\nthunderstorms will likely continue until near sunrise Wednesday\nacross north Mississippi. Storms are expected to be progressive\nand move swiftly through the region. Due to projected storm\nspeed, the 90th percentile QPF through Thursday morning is less\nthan an inch.\n\nOnce the cold frontal boundary clears the region, subtle\nnorthwest flow aloft will filter in drier and cooler conditions.\nTemperatures in the 80s to lower 90s prevail through the end of\nthe work week, which is leaning slightly below normal for the\nlatter half of July. Unfortunately, subtle upper level ridging\nwill build in from the west for the weekend. This will warm\ntemperatures back up into the 90s area wide by Sunday. The NBM\ndoes bring medium to high probabilities of triple digit heat\nindex values making a return for areas along and west of the\nMississippi River. Long term guidance favors a deep, upper trough\nover Quebec which will strengthen northwest flow aloft and could\nkeep the Mid-South in a troughing pattern to close out July.\n\nDNM",
